title: Mass operations in the log
tags: Storage [[Event log]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A write adding a value to, removing it from, or setting a field of many records, or deleting them, is one entry in the log, which reads as one operation per record; no record is read to write it.

A write that adds one value to a field of ten thousand records, or removes it, used
to read each record to log what it held and to write one operation, its two snapshots
and three index keys for it. It is one entry ([[Log storage]]): the field, the value,
and the records that ''changed'' — those that lacked the value, or held it. Which ones
those are comes from the holders of the value ([[Mass writes in the store]]), not from
the records. An entry has one form whatever its number of targets, so nothing in the
log tells a mass operation from an individual one: what is particular is how the write finds its
targets and its before without reading the records.

The write names its records by ''dense id'': the query ends with a bitmap, and the
write takes it as it is (`Writer::mass_add_ids`, `mass_remove_ids`; the routes of
`field add` and `field remove` and the batch endpoint). No record and no existence
check is read per record; the entry's targets are the uuids of the ids, read in the
order of the ids (`tests/kv_mass_log.rs` counts the keys). A call that has uuids
(`mass_add`) looks their ids up, once each.

A write to many records moves HEAD by the number of records it changed, one
operation id each.

!! A set, an unset

`field set` and `field unset` on a query log one entry too, with the values that
replace the field's (none for `unset`). They forget what they replace, so the before
keeps it, grouped by value: for each value the field held, the ranks of the targets
that held it, a `nothing` included. The write finds them without a record per id: the
postings of the field (`nothing` has one), the pending index writes
([[Mass writes in the store]]), and one record per ''distinct value'' found, to give
the value back (a key is not always its value: an int and a float of one number share
it). The cost is that of the field's vocabulary — the number of its distinct values —
not of the records written. The types' counts (`field_types`) are kept from the
counters: the rows that go are the sum of the field's counts over the records.

An add's before is the targets that held `nothing` — the `nothing` posting met with
the targets —, a remove has none.

!! A deletion

Deleting many records (`POST /repos/:repo/query/delete`, and the trash's
`POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/trash`) is one entry of effect `delete`, read as a
`delete_metarecord` per record. The records are ''not'' touched: their ids leave
`live_metarecords`, which every answer is sifted through
([[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]), and a pending write of kind `delete` says that
the records hold the before of the entry ([[Mass writes in the store]]). While it is
pending, the log reads the before of a target in its record; the fold copies it into
the entry's chunks.

What cannot be sifted is kept exact at the write, without reading a record: the type
counts (a sum over the counters of every field), and the parents that lost their last
child (`index_parents`, from the parent holders of each position field, the writes the
transaction made before read as they stand). The histograms count rows and are not:
they give the rows back at the fold, and `mf repo check` folds first.

A folder deleted with every record under it is deleted so, whatever the depth: all of
them leave `live_metarecords` together, and the dead ids that stay in its ancestors'
descendants are sifted like any other. A folder that keeps a child is not — the
children would be left under a node that is gone, which the commit refuses for a
`tree_ref`, and their subtree would leave its ancestors' descendants, which nothing
sifts — so the records are deleted one by one, as before.

The trash first asks, once, which of its records hold a link to a duplicate group (the
holders of the field, among its ids), and only those leave their group one by one
([[Duplicates]]); the others are in the entry, whose origin is the trash's.

!!! Reverting a mass add or remove

A revert of at least sixteen consecutive operations of one mass add or remove of a
field is one entry too (`Writer::revert_mass_run`): a remove of the value from the same
records for an add, an add for a remove, whose operation `first + k` reverts
`reverted + k`. It is done when every record changed is one the revert changes — each
still holds the value an add put, or lacks the one a remove took, and is still alive;
if not, the revert goes one record at a time. Reverting such an entry is the same
mass operation the other way.

!!! Giving a pending deletion back

An undo of a deletion still pending does not recreate the records one by one: they
never left the store, so `Writer::undelete_pending` gives their ids back to
`live_metarecords` — with the type counts and the parents the deletion took off. The
records keep their dense ids and no row or index entry is written; the log gets one
entry of effect `create` on those records, reverting the deletion's operations. Its
pending `delete` stays: it is what says the before is still in the records, until a
write to one of them folds it for that record first. It is done when:

* a run of at least sixteen operations of one pending deletion is reverted;
* the state is the one the deletion left: every operation written since is itself in the revert, and ahead of it;
* no record is alive already, no parent of a given-back record is gone, and every field they hold still has a type counted.

A deletion the fold has already copied into the log is given back in one pass too,
from its before: `mass_undelete_log` writes each record under its own dense id again
(`ids` keeps it, and the index entries filed under the id were never dropped), as a
write by hand of the whole record — newer than every pending write of its part, which
would otherwise apply to it again —, gives its rows back to the histograms, and does
what the pending case does. Besides the three conditions above, each record must be as
the fold left it — its id still its uuid's, not live — and no position it held taken by
a live record since. After a reindex, the records of a deletion have no id any more:
they come back one by one, with new ones.

Anything else — a few records, a write in between, a taken position — goes one by one
as before. Undoing the undo is then an ordinary deletion of each record.

So the rows a deletion took are always known: the record holds them until something
would change them, which copies them into the log first. Reverting a deletion puts
back what it took, not what the record holds now.

!! Pruning and retention

Retention and the prunes take ''whole entries'': a mass write is one unit of history,
as it is one command, and the bound on the log is the bound on its entries
([[Automatic retention]]).

!! The schema check

A write by id is checked against the schema ''once'', after its last operation (the
last of a batch), and without reading a record: the write transaction keeps the
counters of each field and the postings of each value up to date as it writes, so the
records the write changed that may break a constraint on the field
([[Schema constraints]]) are set arithmetic on them —

* the type: a value the write puts in the field is of the wrong type — known from the command, not from the records — and every changed record the constraint targets is named;
* the number of rows: the counters say which records hold more than `max`, or fewer than `min`, rows of the field, and the `nothing` posting which hold a `nothing`;
* the targets: a typed constraint looks only at the changed records holding one of its types in `mf_schema`.

Only the records named are read, and validated as a write to one record is. The check
costs what the write does, whatever the size of the repository, and a repository full
of violations elsewhere costs it nothing. (It once asked the questions of the schema
check — `POST /schema/check` — of the write transaction, which answers them by
reading every row of the field and every record: a few tags added took a second on a
large repository.) A store without counters validates each changed record.

`tests/kv_mass_log.rs` holds the reads of an entry of many targets to those of the
operations it stands for.
